          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Rolling epoxy takes a couple of hours. Everything that decides whether it stays on the floor happens in the days before you open the kit. Treat this as a schedule, not a list of tips, and do the steps in order.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="two-weeks-out" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Two Weeks Out: Find Out What Your Slab Will Allow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying anything, spend an evening finding out what kind of floor you have. These checks cost almost nothing and they decide which product to buy, how much prep to plan, and whether a film coating is a good idea at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Moisture.</strong> Tape a square of clear plastic sheeting, about 18 inches on a side, flat to the bare concrete with every edge sealed. Leave it for 24 hours, ideally on a warm day. If droplets form underneath or the concrete darkens, vapor is coming through the slab and a coating will be at risk. Stop here and plan for a vapor-mitigating primer approved by your coating maker, or consider <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">interlocking tiles</a> instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Existing finishes.</strong> Drip water in several spots. If it soaks in and darkens the concrete within a minute or so, the surface is open. If it beads, a sealer, curing compound or old coating is present and will need to be ground off. If the floor is painted, press duct tape firmly onto it and rip it off. Any paint that comes up means the old finish must go before you coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Age and temperature.</strong> New concrete needs to cure before coating; the usual guidance is at least 28 days, and some makers ask for more. Check the forecast for your planned coating day and the day after. Most kits want the slab, not just the air, within roughly 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit, and well above the dew point. An infrared thermometer settles the question in seconds.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If water beads, epoxy will not bond.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Whatever product you buy, the prepared floor must absorb water quickly. Do the water drop test after every prep step, and do not open the kit until the whole floor passes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="shopping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Tools and Materials to Have on Hand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy everything before prep day so a missing roller cover does not stop work halfway through. The list below assumes a two-part kit and a two-car garage; adjust quantities for your floor. If you have not yet chosen a product, our ranking of <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy kits</a> compares the main options, and our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">cost breakdown</a> shows what a full shopping list usually adds up to.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why you need it</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coating kits plus one spare</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Porous slabs absorb more than the label assumes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete degreaser and stiff brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil blocks adhesion and acid will not remove it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rented diamond grinder with vacuum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most reliable way to create a surface profile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack and pit filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Epoxy or polyurea patch compatible with the coating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drill with mixing paddle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hand stirring rarely mixes two parts fully</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/8-inch non-shedding roller covers, frames, extension pole</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Even film without lint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Angled brush and painter's tape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cutting in along walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spiked shoes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Walking on wet coating to broadcast flakes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Infrared thermometer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Checking slab temperature against the label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Respirator, nitrile gloves, eye protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Follow the product's safety data sheet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Choose roller covers labeled for epoxy or as non-shedding. Cheap covers leave fibers in the floor that are impossible to remove once it cures. Buy several, because you will want a fresh cover for the topcoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the safety data sheet of your product for respirator requirements. Water-based kits are mild, but solvent-borne coatings and some primers need an organic vapor respirator and serious ventilation. Keep children and pets away, and turn off pilot lights on gas water heaters or furnaces in an attached garage if the product label calls for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clean-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Prep Day, Morning: Empty, Degrease and Rinse
          </h2>

          <p>
            Empty the garage completely. Every box, shelf and tool that stays will be in the way of the grinder and will collect dust. Remove anything stored along the walls so you can reach the edges, and take down items hanging low on the walls that could fall onto a wet floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweep thoroughly, then treat every oil and fluid stain. Apply a concrete degreaser, scrub hard with a stiff brush, and let it dwell for the time on the label. Stubborn stains may need several rounds or a poultice. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers the methods that work for older stains. Grinding alone does not remove oil that has soaked deep into the slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse the whole floor and squeegee the water out of the garage. A pressure washer with a surface cleaner attachment speeds this up and leaves a more even result; see our list of <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaners</a> if you own a pressure washer. Rinse twice if you used a strong degreaser, because residue interferes with adhesion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the slab dry fully before grinding. Wet concrete clogs diamond tooling and turns dust into slurry. A fan and an open door help; in humid weather it may take until the next day. Use that time to plan your coating layout and practice mixing proportions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="profile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Prep Day, Afternoon: Grind or Etch the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The goal of this step is texture. Epoxy grips concrete mechanically, so the surface needs to feel roughly like medium sandpaper. Smooth, shiny concrete will not hold any coating long term.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grinding: the reliable route</h3>

          <p>
            A rented walk-behind diamond grinder with a vacuum attached removes the weak top layer of concrete, old sealers and contaminants, and leaves a consistent profile. Work in overlapping passes across the garage, then along the length. Use a handheld angle grinder with a diamond cup wheel and dust shroud for the edges and corners the big machine cannot reach. Vacuum as you go.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grinding creates fine silica dust, which is a respiratory hazard. Use a vacuum rated for concrete dust, wear a properly fitted respirator rated for silica, and keep the garage door partly open. Do not dry sweep the dust; vacuum it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Etching: only for porous slabs</h3>

          <p>
            Acid or citric etching can work on older, rough, porous concrete that already absorbs water quickly. Mix as directed, spread it with a plastic watering can, scrub, and rinse thoroughly. It will not open a smooth or sealed slab. If water still beads after etching, you must grind. After either method, do the water drop test across the whole floor, especially near the door and in tire lanes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Repairs, Vacuuming and Masking
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the surface profiled, every crack and pit is easy to see. Fill them now. Use an epoxy or polyurea crack filler compatible with your coating, rather than a cement-based patch that may not bond well to the coating. Overfill slightly, let it cure, then grind or sand it flush so it does not telegraph through the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decide what to do with control joints, the straight saw-cut lines in the slab. Many installers fill them for a smooth floor, accepting that a crack may reappear along the joint as the slab moves. Others coat into them and leave them open. Either approach works; filling them looks cleaner but may show hairline cracks later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vacuum the entire floor thoroughly, including corners and the edges along walls. Then go over it again. Any dust left behind becomes a weak layer between concrete and coating. Some installers finish by wiping the floor with a lightly damp microfiber mop; let it dry completely afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mask the walls a few inches above the floor and tape off the bottom of the garage door frame, steps and any drain. Lay plastic across doorways into the house. Plan your exit route: you will coat from the back wall toward the garage door, finishing outside.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Coating Day: Mixing Without Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick a day with a slab temperature inside the label range and stable weather. Morning is usually best in summer, when the slab is cooler and pot life longer; in spring and fall, wait until the slab warms. Check the dew point. If the slab is within about five degrees of it, wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the mixing instructions twice. Two-part epoxies need the resin and hardener combined in exactly the ratio the maker specifies, which is why most kits come pre-measured. Pour the hardener into the resin, scrape the sides of the containers, and mix with a drill and paddle at low speed for the time on the label, typically a few minutes. Scrape the sides and bottom again and mix once more. Unmixed material leaves soft, sticky spots that never cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some water-based kits specify an induction time, a rest period after mixing before application. Follow it. High-solids and 100% solids systems usually do not, and they start working immediately. Pour them out of the bucket onto the floor or into a tray right away, because a mass of mixed material in a pail heats up and gels faster.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Check your label for these four numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Ratio</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Exact mix proportion of A to B</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Induction</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rest time after mixing, if any</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Pot life</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Working time once mixed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Recoat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Window for topcoat</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Never mix more than you can apply within the pot life. For fast systems, mix partial kits only if the maker allows it and you can measure precisely. If a batch starts to thicken or feel warm, stop using it. Rolling partially cured epoxy leaves ridges that will not level out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rolling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Coating Day: Cutting In, Rolling and Flakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work with a helper if possible: one person cuts in edges with a brush while the other rolls. Start at the back wall. Brush a strip a few inches wide along the walls and around posts, then roll the field in sections of roughly four by four feet, or as the label suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour a ribbon of coating onto the floor, spread it with the roller, then back-roll in one direction to smooth it. Keep a wet edge, overlapping each new section into the still-wet previous one. Watch for thin spots, especially near the door and in tire lanes where wear will be greatest. The label coverage figure gives a guide: if you have used only half a kit for the area that kit should cover, you are rolling too thin.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are adding decorative flakes, broadcast them while the coating is still wet, typically within the window the label gives. Wear spiked shoes so you can walk on the wet surface. Throw flakes upward so they fall evenly instead of clumping. Do a section at a time, keeping pace with the roller. For full coverage, broadcast until no base coat shows; you will scrape and vacuum the excess once cured.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work toward the garage door and finish outside. Remove the tape from the walls while the coating is still wet for a clean line. Close the door partway to keep leaves and insects out while leaving ventilation, and put up a sign so no one walks in. Clean tools according to the product type: water for water-based kits, the specified solvent for others.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="topcoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Topcoat Day: Clear Coat and Traction
          </h2>

          <p>
            A clear topcoat is optional in some kits but strongly recommended over a water-based base. It adds a wear layer, locks in flakes and, if UV-stable, prevents yellowing from sunlight. Apply it within the recoat window on the label. Too soon and you can disturb the base; too late and the base may need sanding for the topcoat to grip.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you used a full flake broadcast, scrape the cured surface with a floor scraper to knock off loose and standing flakes, then vacuum thoroughly. This step decides how smooth the final floor feels. Skip it, and the topcoat will lock in sharp edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix an anti-slip additive into the topcoat. It is a fine grit that gives traction when the floor is wet, which matters on a garage floor where cars track in rain and snow. Stir it often while rolling, because it settles. Use a fresh, non-shedding roller cover and apply in thin, even coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Topcoats tend to be thinner and faster to apply than base coats, but they show roller marks more clearly. Work in good light, keep a wet edge, and roll in a consistent direction. A flashlight held low to the floor reveals missed spots.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. The Cure Week and What Can Go Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy feels dry to the touch long before it is cured. Most kits allow light foot traffic after about a day and vehicle traffic after roughly three to seven days at moderate temperatures. Cool weather slows curing considerably, so add time in spring or fall. Polycuramine and polyaspartic products are usually faster; follow the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the floor clean and dry during the cure week. Do not drag anything across it, set heavy items on it, or let water sit on it. When you finally park, avoid turning the steering wheel while stationary for the first few weeks, and consider a mat under each tire if your car runs hot after a long drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Common problems and fixes</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Bubbles</strong> usually come from air escaping from porous concrete as the slab warms, a process called outgassing. Coating on a cooling slab, late in the day, reduces it. <strong>Fisheyes</strong>, small craters where the coating pulls away, point to contamination such as oil or silicone. <strong>Cloudy or greasy patches</strong> can indicate amine blush from humidity on some epoxies; wash and sand before recoating. <strong>Soft spots</strong> usually mean incomplete mixing and must be scraped out and patched.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance afterward is simple: sweep often, mop with a mild cleaner and wipe up spills promptly. If the floor needs refreshing after a few years, a light sanding and a new topcoat restore gloss on a well-bonded base. If you are weighing other chemistries for your next floor, our <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">coating comparison</a> covers polyaspartic and polycuramine options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a slab that fails the water drop test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If water beads on any part of the prepared floor, something is still sealing the surface. Coating over it almost guarantees peeling in that area. Grind those spots again and retest until every part of the floor absorbs water quickly. It is tedious, but it is far easier than removing a failed coating later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring by hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two-part epoxy only cures where resin and hardener have combined. Hand stirring often leaves unmixed streaks near the bottom and sides of the bucket, which become soft, sticky spots in the floor. Use a drill and paddle at low speed, scrape the sides, and mix for the full time the label specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling too thin to stretch the kit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spreading a kit over more area than its label covers leaves a thin film that wears through quickly in tire lanes. Check your progress: the amount used should match the area coated according to the coverage figure. Buy an extra kit rather than stretching material to finish the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating as the slab warms up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cold slab warming in the morning sun pushes air out of the concrete, which rises as bubbles in the fresh coating. Coat when the slab temperature is steady or falling, usually later in the day, and keep the garage door partly closed to avoid direct sun on the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on it too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A floor that feels hard is not necessarily cured. Warm tires on an under-cured coating can leave permanent marks or pull it up entirely. Wait the full time on the label, add more in cool weather, and treat the first few weeks gently by avoiding stationary steering and heavy point loads.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many days does a DIY epoxy garage floor take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on at least two days of work, often three: one for emptying, degreasing and grinding, one for repairs and the base coat, and sometimes another for the topcoat. Then add three to seven days of cure before parking. Spreading the work over two weekends is common and sensible. Larger garages take longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I skip grinding and just use the etch in the kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on older, porous concrete that absorbs water quickly. Most garage slabs are smooth and dense, and many have sealers or curing compounds that etching cannot remove. If water beads after etching, you need to grind. Grinding is the most reliable way to get a lasting bond. Rental grinders are widely available.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is cold or humid weather a problem for epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most kits need a slab temperature of roughly 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit and a slab well above the dew point. Cold slows curing and can prevent a full cure; humidity can cause blush or poor adhesion. Check both before mixing. Polyaspartic products tolerate cooler temperatures but cure very fast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fill the control joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a choice. Filling gives a smoother, cleaner look but a crack may reappear along the joint as the slab moves seasonally. Leaving them open avoids that but collects dirt. Many owners fill them with a flexible joint filler for appearance and accept occasional hairline cracks. Either way, decide before coating day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I avoid lap marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep a wet edge by working in small sections and overlapping into still-wet coating. Work with a helper so one person cuts in edges while the other rolls. Do not go back over areas that have started to set. Good lighting helps you see thin spots and missed areas. Plan the layout before you mix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a topcoat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over a water-based base coat, a topcoat is strongly recommended because the base is thin. It adds a wear layer, locks in flakes and, with a UV-stable product, prevents yellowing. Over a thick 100% solids base, it is optional but still helps with gloss, traction and stain resistance. Apply it within the label's recoat window.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I epoxy over a painted garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the paint is well bonded and compatible, which is rare with old floor paint. Usually the paint must be ground off. Do a firm tape test in several spots; if paint lifts, remove it. Coating over weak paint bonds the new layer to a surface that is already failing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

